  The cost curve does NOT turn over. Operating cost falls monotonically to 5 cycles, the last feasible point.
  PHYSICAL ceiling : 6 cycles -- binding mineral SI_silica_am. Acid cannot move it: sulfate saturation is not pH-sensitive,
  so the lever that buys cycles against calcite stops working entirely at this wall.

  This is the more dangerous case. The economics point straight at a hard limit, and the Langelier index the industry controls on
  describes calcite only -- it cannot represent silica_am at all. An operator following the money with LSI-based control walks into the wall blind.

